Sure, here's an R code that takes two strings as input and determines if they are anagrams or not:
```
are_anagrams <- function(str1, str2) {
  # Convert strings to lowercase and remove spaces
  str1 <- tolower(gsub(" ", "", str1))
  str2 <- tolower(gsub(" ", "", str2))

  # Check if the length of the strings are equal
  if (nchar(str1) != nchar(str2)) {
    return(FALSE)
  }

  # Sort the characters in both strings
  str1_sorted <- paste(sort(strsplit(str1, "")[[1]]), collapse="")
  str2_sorted <- paste(sort(strsplit(str2, "")[[1]]), collapse="")

  # Check if the sorted strings are equal
  if (str1_sorted == str2_sorted) {
    return(TRUE)
  } else {
    return(FALSE)
  }
}
# Example usage
str1 <- "listen"
str2 <- "silent"
if (are_anagrams(str1, str2)) {
  print("The two strings are anagrams.")
} else {
  print("The two strings are not anagrams.")
}
```
In this code, we first convert the strings to lowercase and remove any spaces. Then, we check if the length of the strings are equal. If not, we return `FALSE` as they cannot be anagrams. If the lengths are equal, we sort the characters in both strings and check if the sorted strings are equal. If they are, we return `TRUE` as the strings are anagrams. Otherwise, we return `FALSE`.

