To solve the system of equations with three variables (price, advertising cost, and time of year) to predict sales for any given combination of these variables, we can use matrix algebra. The system of equations can be represented in matrix form as Ax = b, where A is the matrix of coefficients, x is the vector of unknowns, and b is the vector of constants.
Here is an example of the LaTeX code for the matrix equation:
$$\begin{pmatrix}
p_{11} & p_{12} & p_{13}\\
p_{21} & p_{22} & p_{23}\\
p_{31} & p_{32} & p_{33}\\
\end{pmatrix}
\begin{pmatrix}
x_1\\
x_2\\
x_3\\
\end{pmatrix}
=
\begin{pmatrix}
b_1\\
b_2\\
b_3\\
\end{pmatrix}$$
where p is the matrix of coefficients, x is the vector of unknowns (price, advertising cost, and time of year), and b is the vector of constants (sales).
To implement the system of equations in PHP, we can use the PHP linear algebra library, which provides functions for solving systems of linear equations. Here is an example of the PHP code:
```php
<?php
use MathPHP\LinearAlgebra\Matrix;
use MathPHP\LinearAlgebra\Vector;
// Define the matrix of coefficients
$p = new Matrix([
    [1, 2, 3],
    [4, 5, 6],
    [7, 8, 9],
]);
// Define the vector of constants
$b = new Vector([10, 11, 12]);
// Solve the system of equations to get the vector of unknowns
$x = $p->solve($b);
// Print the vector of unknowns
print_r($x);
?>
```
