How can I modify the given C++ code to de-identify text by replacing potentially identifying words with placeholders and store the results in an Excel table using Swift? Here is an example table:
| Original Text                              | De-Identified Text             |
|--------------------------------------------|--------------------------------|
| My sister Kathryn attended the University  | My sister [Placeholder] attended|
| of Washington in Seattle.                   | the University of Washington   |
Here is the modified Swift code snippet that can accomplish this:
import Foundation
let inputFile = "input.txt"
let outputFile = "output.csv"
do {
    let inputString = try String(contentsOfFile: inputFile, encoding: .utf8)
    var outputString = ""
    // Split input string into lines
    let lines = inputString.split(separator: "\n")
    for line in lines {
        var words = line.split(separator: " ")
        var deIdentifiedWords = [String]()
        // Check each word in the line
        for word in words {
            // Check if word is potentially identifying
            if word == "Kathryn" || word == "Seattle" {
                deIdentifiedWords.append("[Placeholder]")
            } else {
                deIdentifiedWords.append(String(word))
            }
        }
        // Convert de-identified words to CSV format
        let deIdentifiedLine = deIdentifiedWords.joined(separator: ",") + "\n"
        // Append de-identified line to output string
        outputString.append(deIdentifiedLine)
    }
    // Write output string to file
    try outputString.write(toFile: outputFile, atomically: true, encoding: .utf8)
} catch {
    print("Error: \(error.localizedDescription)")
}
This Swift code reads in a file called "input.txt" and writes the de-identified data to a CSV file called "output.csv". It checks each word in each line to see if it is potentially identifying, and if so, replaces it with "[Placeholder]". The de-identified words are then converted to CSV format and written to the output file.

