One efficient mechanism to guarantee that every generated random string is unique and hasn't been created before is to use a map data structure to keep track of the generated strings. 
Each time a new string is generated, it can be checked against the map to see if it already exists. If it does, then another string can be generated until a unique one is found. If the map is implemented efficiently, this process should not compromise performance significantly.
Here is an example implementation:
```go
package main
import (
    "fmt"
    "math/rand"
    "time"
)
const (
    charset = "abcdefghijklmnopqrstuvwxyzABCDEFGHIJKLMNOPQRSTUVWXYZ0123456789"
    length  = 10
)
var seededRand *rand.Rand = rand.New(
    rand.NewSource(time.Now().UnixNano()))
func generateRandomString() string {
    for {
        b := make([]byte, length)
        for i := range b {
            b[i] = charset[seededRand.Intn(len(charset))]
        }
        str := string(b)
        if _, found := stringMap[str]; !found {
            stringMap[str] = true
            return str
        }
    }
}
var stringMap = make(map[string]bool)
func main() {
    for i := 0; i < 100; i++ {
        fmt.Println(generateRandomString())
    }
}
```
In this implementation, the `generateRandomString()` function generates a random string and checks if it already exists in the `stringMap`. If it does, it generates another string until a unique one is found. If the string is unique, it is added to the `stringMap`.
The `stringMap` is a map with string keys and boolean values. It keeps track of which strings have been generated before, allowing the function to efficiently check if a string is unique or not.
This implementation should be efficient because the `stringMap` is implemented as a hash table, which allows for constant-time lookups and insertions. The `generateRandomString()` function will continue generating strings until a unique one is found, but this should not significantly impact performance because the probability of generating a duplicate string is low.

