One of the most popular sorting algorithms is the Quicksort algorithm. It has an average time complexity of O(n log n) and a worst-case time complexity of O(n^2). Its space complexity is O(log n) on average and O(n) in the worst case.
Here is the Python code for implementing the Quicksort algorithm to sort the given list of integers in ascending order:
```
def quicksort(numbers):
    if len(numbers) <= 1:
        return numbers
    else:
        pivot = numbers[0]
        left = []
        right = []
        for i in range(1, len(numbers)):
            if numbers[i] < pivot:
                left.append(numbers[i])
            else:
                right.append(numbers[i])
        return quicksort(left) + [pivot] + quicksort(right)
numbers = [5, 3, 9, 1, 4]
sorted_numbers = quicksort(numbers)
print(sorted_numbers)
```
In this implementation, we first check if the length of the list is less than or equal to 1. If it is, we return the list as it is already sorted. Otherwise, we choose the first element of the list as the pivot and partition the remaining elements into two sub-lists: one containing elements less than the pivot and the other containing elements greater than or equal to the pivot.
We then recursively apply the same partitioning process to the left and right sub-lists until we reach the base case of a sub-list with length less than or equal to 1. Finally, we concatenate the sorted left sub-list, the pivot, and the sorted right sub-list to obtain the sorted list.
As mentioned earlier, the Quicksort algorithm has an average time complexity of O(n log n) and a worst-case time complexity of O(n^2). However, in practice, it is one of the fastest sorting algorithms and is often used in programming languages like Python for sorting large datasets.

