To pause the song after 3 minutes using DispatchQueue, you need to add a code block inside the asyncAfter closure that will pause the song. One way to achieve this is by using another DispatchQueue to execute the pause code block on the main thread, like this:
```
func timedSong(duration: Double, song: () -> ()) {
    let workItem = DispatchWorkItem {
        song()
    }
    DispatchQueue.global().asyncAfter(deadline: .now() + duration, execute: workItem)
    DispatchQueue.global().asyncAfter(deadline: .now() + duration + 3.0) {
        DispatchQueue.main.async {
            workItem.cancel()
        }
    }
}
```
In this updated function, we create a DispatchWorkItem that executes the song code block. We then schedule this work item to run after the specified duration using DispatchQueue.global().asyncAfter(). 
To pause the song after 3 minutes, we create another asyncAfter closure that's scheduled to run 3 seconds after the duration has elapsed. Inside this closure, we use DispatchQueue.main.async() to execute the code block on the main thread, which cancels the work item that's running the song. 
To use this function, you would call it like this:
```
timedSong(duration: 180.0) {
    // Your song code here
}
```
This will time the song to run for 3 minutes and pause it after 3 seconds. If you need to cancel the song before it's paused, you can call the cancel() method on the workItem that's returned by the first asyncAfter() call.

