Java can be used to conduct an analysis of transitional words' frequency and patterns in a given written composition by utilizing natural language processing libraries such as Stanford CoreNLP or Apache OpenNLP. These libraries can be used to parse the text and identify the parts of speech and relationships between words, allowing for the identification of transitional words and their usage patterns.
Here is a sample code that can be used to achieve this task using Stanford CoreNLP:
```
import java.util.*;
import edu.stanford.nlp.ling.*;
import edu.stanford.nlp.pipeline.*;
import edu.stanford.nlp.util.*;
public class TransitionalWordsAnalyzer {
  public static void main(String[] args) {
    // set up pipeline
    Properties props = new Properties();
    props.setProperty("annotators", "tokenize, ssplit, pos, lemma, parse");
    StanfordCoreNLP pipeline = new StanfordCoreNLP(props);
    // input text
    String text = "This is a sample text. It contains some transitional words, such as however, therefore, and furthermore.";
    // annotate text
    Annotation document = new Annotation(text);
    pipeline.annotate(document);
    // get sentences
    List<CoreMap> sentences = document.get(CoreAnnotations.SentencesAnnotation.class);
    // iterate over sentences
    for (CoreMap sentence : sentences) {
      // get tokens and tags
      List<CoreLabel> tokens = sentence.get(CoreAnnotations.TokensAnnotation.class);
      List<String> tags = sentence.get(CoreAnnotations.PartOfSpeechAnnotation.class);
      // iterate over tokens and tags
      for (int i = 0; i < tokens.size(); i++) {
        String token = tokens.get(i).originalText();
        String tag = tags.get(i);
        // check for transitional words
        if (tag.equals("RB") || tag.equals("IN")) {
          System.out.println(token);
        }
      }
    }
  }
}
```
This code utilizes the Stanford CoreNLP library to tokenize the input text, identify the parts of speech and relationships between words, and identify transitional words by their part of speech tags.
To optimize this analysis for different types of writing, such as academic papers and business reports, the analysis can be tailored to identify specific types of transitional words that are commonly used in those types of writing. For example, academic papers may frequently use transitional words such as "however", "nevertheless", and "in contrast", while business reports may frequently use transitional words such as "furthermore", "moreover", and "in addition".
