Sure, here's the Java code implementation for Merge Sort algorithm:
```
public class MergeSort {

    public static void mergeSort(int[] arr, int left, int right) {
        if (left < right) {
            int mid = (left + right) / 2;
            mergeSort(arr, left, mid);
            mergeSort(arr, mid + 1, right);
            merge(arr, left, mid, right);
        }
    }

    public static void merge(int[] arr, int left, int mid, int right) {
        int[] temp = new int[right - left + 1];
        int i = left, j = mid + 1, k = 0;
        while (i <= mid && j <= right) {
            if (arr[i] <= arr[j]) {
                temp[k++] = arr[i++];
            } else {
                temp[k++] = arr[j++];
            }
        }
        while (i <= mid) {
            temp[k++] = arr[i++];
        }
        while (j <= right) {
            temp[k++] = arr[j++];
        }
        for (int x = 0; x < temp.length; x++) {
            arr[left + x] = temp[x];
        }
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int[] arr = {5, 2, 8, 3, 1, 6, 9, 7, 4};
        mergeSort(arr, 0, arr.length - 1);
        for (int i : arr) {
            System.out.print(i + " ");
        }
    }
}
```
Time complexity: O(n log n) - This is because the merge sort algorithm divides the array into two halves recursively until the base case is reached. At each level, the algorithm performs n operations (merging two sorted arrays) and the number of levels is log n. Therefore, the time complexity is O(n log n).
Space complexity: O(n) - This is because the merge sort algorithm creates a temporary array of size n to store the sorted elements during the merging process. The space complexity is proportional to the size of the input array.

