To assign a randomly generated employee number and department code to each new employee and department respectively, you can use the `Random` class in C#. Here's an example of how you can modify the `Employee` and `Department` classes to achieve this:
```csharp
public class Employee
{
    private static Random random = new Random();
    private int employeeNumber;
    public Employee(string name, string position, double salary)
    {
        this.Name = name;
        this.Position = position;
        this.Salary = salary;
        this.employeeNumber = random.Next(1000, 9999); // generate a 4-digit employee number
    }
    public string Name { get; set; }
    public string Position { get; set; }
    public double Salary { get; set; }
    public int EmployeeNumber { get { return employeeNumber; } }
}
public class Department
{
    private static Random random = new Random();
    private int departmentCode;
    private double budget;
    private List<Employee> employees = new List<Employee>();
    public Department(string name, double budget)
    {
        this.Name = name;
        this.budget = budget;
        this.departmentCode = random.Next(100, 999); // generate a 3-digit department code
    }
    public string Name { get; set; }
    public double Budget { get { return budget; } }
    public int DepartmentCode { get { return departmentCode; } }
    public void AddEmployee(Employee employee)
    {
        employees.Add(employee);
        budget -= employee.Salary; // subtract employee's salary from department budget
    }
    public List<Employee> GetEmployees()
    {
        return employees;
    }
}
```
In the `Employee` class, we've added a private field `employeeNumber` and initialized it with a random number between 1000 and 9999 using the `Random.Next()` method. We've also added a public property `EmployeeNumber` that returns the value of `employeeNumber`.
In the `Department` class, we've added a private field `departmentCode` and initialized it with a random number between 100 and 999 using the `Random.Next()` method. We've also added a public property `DepartmentCode` that returns the value of `departmentCode`.
To update the department budget when a new employee is added, we've modified the `AddEmployee()` method to subtract the employee's salary from the department budget.
